      <div class="dt-stats" id="stats"></div>
      <details class="dt-fold dt-fold-note" id="statsNote">
        <summary>How closely to read these numbers</summary>
        <div class="dt-fold-body">
          <p class="dt-stats-note">
            With &mu; known only to a range, read differences under about a
            tenth of a second as noise. Comparisons between two setups in the
            same model are sturdier than any absolute number.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <figure class="dt-sheet dt-sheet-sim">
        <figcaption class="dt-sheet-h">Simulate <span class="dt-sheet-sub">the whole field &middot; real Pedro chains &middot; this drivetrain's grip</span></figcaption>
        <p class="dt-honest">
          Geometry, syntax and the Java export are real Pedro Pathing. The
          timing is this page's own grip envelope, not the tuned follower.
        </p>
        <div class="dt-path-pick">
          <label class="dt-path-sel" for="pvSel">Path</label>
          <select id="pvSel">
            <option value="preload" selected>Quickstart DECODE auto, preload score</option>
            <option value="decode">Quickstart DECODE auto, full chain</option>
            <option value="motif">AprilTag motif auto, PPG branch</option>
            <option value="bucket">Into the Deep bucket auto, legacy 1.0.x syntax</option>
            <option value="circle">Circle example, tangent heading</option>
            <option value="lace">Straight lace, 3 tiles, constant heading</option>
            <option value="custom">Custom: your own chain</option>
          </select>
          <span class="dt-path-sel" id="pvModeCap">Speed model</span>
          <div class="dt-seg dt-seg-sm" role="group" aria-labelledby="pvModeCap">
            <button type="button" id="pvModeGrip" class="is-active" aria-pressed="true">Grip envelope</button>
            <button type="button" id="pvModeFollow" aria-pressed="false">Follower-style</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="dt-models">
          <div class="dt-model" id="pvModelGrip" data-on="true">
            <b>Grip envelope</b>
            What your drivetrain can hold. It slows into every corner to the
            traction limit, so this is the honest time to choose gearing on.
          </div>
          <div class="dt-model" id="pvModelFollow">
            <b>Follower-style</b>
            What Pedro's follower actually commands. Full power along the
            tangent, braking only inside stopping distance of the chain's end,
            and a <b class="dt-model-in">grip exceeded</b> flag wherever a
            corner would break traction.
          </div>
          <p class="dt-models-gap">
            The difference between the two times is the margin the real
            follower is spending: full power through the corners, paid back in
            a long zero-power stop.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="dt-path-grid">
          <div class="dt-path-main">
            <div class="dt-chartbox dt-fieldbox" id="pathBox"><canvas id="cPath" role="img" aria-label="Top-down 144 by 144 inch FTC field of six by six foam tiles, showing the selected Pedro Pathing bezier chain, its control points, the start and end poses, and the robot driving the chain to scale"></canvas></div>
            <div class="dt-anim-controls">
              <button type="button" class="dt-btn dt-btn-sm dt-btn-primary" id="pvPlay">Play</button>
              <button type="button" class="dt-btn dt-btn-sm dt-btn-ghost" id="pvRestart" aria-label="Restart the path">restart</button>
              <label class="dt-anim-speed"><span class="dt-visually-hidden">Path playback speed</span>
